3:Tally takes GitHub issues and a budget, then routes each task to a coding model likely to finish it economically. It watches token spend, verifies the changes with your repository's test command, and can hand failed or ceiling-stopped work to a stronger tier. You get pull requests and a ledger of each attempt, not automatically merged code.
13:For the offline demo and dashboard: **Node.js 20 or newer and npm**. No GitHub login, agent login, API key, or Agent Orchestrator is needed.
21:- Optionally, [Agent Orchestrator](https://docs.orchestrator.inc), installed and running, if you choose `runner: ao`. The direct runner needs no AO daemon.
23:neatlogs, Entire, and cfo.ai are optional. You can leave all three off and still run tasks, verify changes, publish PRs, and inspect the local ledger.
66:Do not use `tally demo --live` as your no-account test: it reads enabled integrations using your credentials, though workers stay simulated and it does not write cfo.ai spend.
113:| Use cfo.ai for budget, spend and Ari context? | Say yes to read the monthly budget, write real spend, add Tally's managed scenario guide, and share per-attempt reports with the selected workspace. Missing spend tables still require a separate confirmation. No keeps the local budget and ledger. |
119:| Monthly budget or local fallback | Without cfo.ai, enter your monthly local budget, which is also the default run cap. With cfo.ai connected, init shows its current monthly figure and asks for a local fallback if reads fail. That fallback does not change the live cfo.ai budget. Always choose a separate small cap for your first run. |
120:| Apply these changes? | Read the repository/base branch, runner, tiers, prices, tests, setup, products, files and AO changes. Say yes to write `tally.yaml` and `.tally/install.json` and apply the listed AO changes. No leaves those final installation changes unapplied. Previously approved integration connection changes are separate, not rolled back by this answer. |
134:The printed allow-list permits worktree file edits, git add/commit/status/diff, and the exact test/setup scripts. Config stores portable, content-bound script tokens, not machine-specific paths. At each worker start, Tally writes the scripts outside the worker's worktree, materialises exact permissions and tells the worker the exact verification-script path. The worker cannot edit those scripts; Tally independently runs the real test command afterwards.
142:**cfo.ai:** Tally uses built-in MCP OAuth, so no separate cfo.ai CLI/package install is needed. If not connected, init offers browser login. Select your workspace and scenario from the numbered choices, or press Enter to skip. It checks the budget and spend tables. Before creating any missing table, init shows the workspace, scenario, rows, starting values, and formula in a readable proposal and asks for approval. It does not reset existing model data. After the cfo.ai opt-in, Tally adds or updates its marked guide on the selected scenario page and enables per-attempt reports in organization memory. The reports are shared with the selected workspace and give Ari each task's model, token/cost assumptions, result, budget snapshot, and PR/checkpoint/trace links. They exclude issue bodies, prompts, source, transcripts, raw trace contents, and credentials. Dry runs never publish attempt reports. An incomplete connection falls back to local budgeting. `tally login cfoai` can reconnect later.
144:To verify Ari's context in a demo, ask for a task's spend and route by issue number, compare the same recorded tokens at another configured tier's rates, and ask for the monthly budget remaining at the report's snapshot time. A grounded answer should cite the attempt/run, show the rate and token assumptions, distinguish estimates from provider invoices, and say when data is missing. A same-token what-if is a price comparison; it does not establish that another model would have produced the same result.
196:Available input flags include `--test-command`, `--setup-command` (an empty string means none), `--issue-label`, `--neatlogs yes|no`, `--entire yes|no`, and `--cfoai yes|no`. `--yes` approves the final detected proposal without prompting; it does not replace missing verification evidence or Claude consent. Scripted Claude setup also needs the explicit `--allow-claude` approval. Use interactive setup first if you are unsure.
237:  Monthly remaining: $40.00 of $40.00 (cfo.ai)
243:These are recorded figures, not your defaults or a cost forecast. The monthly budget came from that cfo.ai account; `--budget 1` set only this run's cap. `small` is the estimated scope; confidence is sizing certainty, not a measured probability of correctness. `cheap` is a tier name from that config, and the ceiling is the maximum intended spend for that attempt, not a promised bill.
259:For `runner: ao`, `--parallel 3` overrides the explicit `parallelWorkers` opt-in for that run. Missing `parallelWorkers` means one worker; the legacy `concurrency` field never enables parallelism, and direct always remains sequential. Init records `parallelWorkers` only from the chosen worker mode. Before starting each parallel task, Tally reserves its ceiling and one next-tier escalation against the run cap and monthly remainder. Tasks wait for other reservations to settle, or are skipped if they cannot fit at all. Paid parallel runs require a readable monthly budget; demo/dry runs can fall back to the configured budget because workers are simulated. Baseline runs stay sequential because they have no per-task ceilings. Usage polling is asynchronous: reservations prevent overcommitting funds, but final usage can overshoot a ceiling between polls. The shared observed-budget guard stops all workers if their combined reported spend reaches the available cap.
261:The local dashboard address is printed before the first worker starts. `--open` opens the browser; `--dashboard` alone serves it without automatically opening it. Routes, worker IDs, periodic spend, attempt results and PR URLs appear in the terminal. The dashboard shows task state and increasing live spend; finished ledger rows appear after attempts end, not as partial rows for a running attempt.
268:#17 attempt 1: spend $0.0029
269:#17 attempt 1: spend $0.0061
270:#17 attempt 1: spend $0.0109
272:  Monthly remaining: $40.00 of $40.00 (cfo.ai)
277:  Total spend: $0.0109
292:Permission/infrastructure errors are not cured by blindly spending on a stronger model. A sustained request for human input, missing checkout, or unavailable live meter is reported and the worker is closed. A recovered Claude command refusal can be a warning rather than a task failure: Tally's own verification is the judge. Finished workers are closed on success and failure paths.
294:Ceilings are **polling-based safety thresholds, not billing guarantees**: usage arrives at model-call/message boundaries and stopping can lag. Actual spend may exceed a threshold by in-flight usage, and Tally records it rather than clipping the ledger. Setup time is recorded separately; test/setup commands and external infrastructure may have costs outside model-token accounting. Keep margin below your account-level spending limit.
307:| cfo.ai | Configured monthly `Agent budget`, `Agent spend`, `Budget remaining`, and tier rows in the spend-detail table. Dashboard/run state retain available page/table links. Real spend is written after attempts; a failed write is exposed with `cfoai-spend-fallback.csv`, not silently treated as success. |
309:Costs are list-price token estimates, not provider invoices or subscription cash charges. Changing prices does not reprice past ledgers. With cfo.ai off, no model cells are read or written; with neatlogs off, no decision traces are exported; with Entire off, no checkpoint evidence is promised.
319:| Direct / Claude Code | Tally starts `claude -p`, meters streamed usage including cache writes, host-commits edits and publishes. | [#14](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/14) |
322:Direct Codex uses a workspace-write sandbox with network disabled and no bypass/full-access mode. Direct Claude uses consented `dontAsk` tool rules, not that same OS sandbox. Both receive Tally's exact verification script; Tally handles publishing. Usage and trace evidence are separate: a worker can be metered without having delivered its neatlogs trace.
328:| Agent missing or signed out | Install the selected CLI and run `claude auth login` or `codex login`, then doctor. Tally stops rather than substituting another harness. Login alone does not establish entitlement to a particular model. |
332:| `ao` not on PATH in a normal terminal | Use direct, or set `TALLY_AO_BIN` to the installed AO executable. On macOS Tally also looks inside the Agent Orchestrator app bundle; doctor and init use the same resolver. |
334:| GitHub timeout/reset/5xx | Read-only intake and PR discovery retry network-class failures up to three attempts with a printed retry line. Auth/not-found errors and publishing writes are not blindly retried. Follow the surfaced `gh` diagnostic command; check auth/network before retrying a run that might already have published. |
336:| Larger-than-expected route or budget skip | Inspect named target files, confidence, test coverage, selected tiers and ceilings. `--budget` is a run cap; the monthly cfo.ai budget is separate. The next escalation ceiling must fit too. |
341:| cfo.ai unreachable or write failed | Check `tally login cfoai` and doctor; review the displayed fallback/source. Keep the CSV fallback and reconcile before changing cells manually or rerunning work. |
343:| Init fails after an approved operation | Read its explicit changed/unwritten list and recovery command. Re-init preserves owned matching files; it refuses to overwrite unrelated edits. Optional integration side effects have their own approvals. |
365:It lists owned config/installation files and AO registration/hooks/settings, then asks default-No confirmation. It refuses to overwrite user changes or remove a project with active sessions. If you edited the config after init, preserve it and follow the refusal message rather than deleting ownership records.

